# Haskell practices

Deeper notes: skill `haskell-canon` → [lessons.md](../skills/haskell-canon/lessons.md).

## Math structure ≅ code structure (compositional)

This is the primary reason to write this project in Haskell rather than a
conventional numeric language. **The implementation should look like the
mathematics**, composed of the same pieces with the same names and laws—not a
procedural simulation that happens to compute the right numbers.

- If the maths is a (fusion / compact closed / monoidal) category, the code
  should be morphisms, tensor, cups/caps, unitors, F-moves, … composed with
  `(.)` / `(⊗^)` / `trace`—not index loops and ad-hoc buffers that “do the
  same thing.”
- Prefer definitions that are readable as the paper’s formula or diagram
  (short compositions of library morphisms and constructors) over unpacking
  into arrays and rebuilding.
- When code drifts from the math presentation, treat that as a design bug:
  missing combinator, wrong domain, or an invented wrapper—not something to
  paper over with more helpers.
- Unfused cups use **right duals**: @ε : a ⊗ a* → I@ ('cupUnfusedObj'),
  @η : I → a* ⊗ a@ ('capUnfusedObj'). Hom packing is Dual-left, so @id = η@.
  Bridging @η@ to @ε@ needs an explicit braid (@swapMap@), not a silent type lie.
- Fused 'HomFused' identity is @'idHomFTrees' (ObjFTrees a)@: position-diagonal
  leaf ids on cartesian 'FuseFTrees' — not an 'Obj'-indexed method class.

Anti-boilerplate, library-first constructors, and “don’t unpack buffers” below
are consequences of this principle.

## Parse, don't validate

([Alexis King](https://lexi-lambda.github.io/blog/2019/11/05/parse-don-t-validate/)):
a check that returns `()` / only throws **discards the proof**. Prefer parsers
that return a refined type. Strengthen arguments (`NonEmpty`, typed morphisms)
instead of weakening every callee to `Maybe` and re-handling “impossible”
cases. Push proof upward to the boundary; no shotgun parsing (don’t act on
half-validated input). Suspect `m ()` validators whose job is erroring.

```haskell
-- BAD: validate, throw proof away, re-check downstream
assertShape v >> applyLoose v

-- GOOD: parse once; callees require the refined type
applyHeff = leftEnv .$ siteMap .$ rightEnv
```

Compose typed morphisms (`.$`, `⊗^`, `trace`, unitors, `siteDagger`). Runtime
checks belong at IO / array boundaries and in `*.Reference` — not in the core
TN path. Brand or rank-2-quantify session-like values when mixing them would be
a real bug (ad `forall s` / bound `Var` / `Scope`).

## Scheme vs algebra

For recursive structure (N-site folds, env accumulation, transfers): name the
recursion scheme once and write a non-recursive algebra. Do not hand-roll
`fmap`-style recursion at every call site (recursion-schemes lesson).

## API shape

- Prefer a small family with systematic variants over one-off names (ad-style
  suffixes / Fixed↔General extensions).
- Prefer final, composable encodings (pass capabilities / morphisms) over
  closed case-splits on representation (discrimination lesson).
- Power-to-weight: do not add heavy type machinery unless it kills a real bug
  class (bound: Derived > Overkill).
- Keep partial/unsafe helpers off the production import path (ad Internal).

## Anti-boilerplate

- Prefer a short composition that reuses existing helpers over a long
  case / list / index solution.
- If a small idea needs a lot of plumbing, **stop and redesign**.
- Do not invent parallel APIs that duplicate Fixed / General / Reference with
  slight renames.
- In Hom / fusion-object code, import `Obj` constructors unqualified
  (`Irrep`, `(:⊗:)`, `(:⊕:)`) and write `'Irrep j`, `a :⊗: b`, `a :⊕: b` —
  not `'FObj.Irrep` / `Tensor` / `Sum`.

### Call sites before names

Before exporting a helper, grep callers. If nothing in the production path uses
it (only a smoke, or only the helper’s own round-trip test), **delete or inline**
— do not keep a named API “for clarity.” Thin unwraps of an existing morphism
(`case f x of …`) belong at the use site, not as a parallel entry point.
Smoke-only adapters belong in `*.Smoke` / `*.Examples`, not in production modules.

**Proactive (do not wait to be asked):** when a general form lands, delete the
specialized synonym in the same edit (`idHomI` once `idHomFTrees` exists;
`unitLunit` once `lunit` is in scope). One-use local helpers that only name a
literal matrix / one-liner should be inlined at their single call site.

### Prefer library instances and constructors

Reach for what `linearmap-category` / hmatrix Static already provide before
hand-rolling:

- Morphisms: `arr (LinearFunction …)`, `(⊗)`, `trace`, unitors, `idTensor`,
  `fromLinearForm`, `asTensor` / `fromTensor`
- Static packing: `Sized` (`konst`, `fromList`, `extract`, `create`) and the
  `Tensor` constructor — e.g. a unit scalar as `Tensor . konst`, not an ad-hoc
  `⊗` of two length-1 vectors plus `unsafeFromArray`
- Dual / metric structure: existing `InnerSpace` / `vectorConjugate` /
  `hermitianNorm`-style maps — do not invent domain-specific dual wrappers that
  restate those

### No synonym wrappers for existing morphisms

Do **not** export `foo = bar` renames of library / categorical morphisms
(`unitLunit = lunit`, `unitRunit = runit`, …). Call `lunit` / `runit` /
`lunitInv` / `runitInv` (or the class methods `idl` / `idr`) directly.

A new name is warranted only when it **adds a constraint, law, or domain** the
underlying name does not already express (e.g. `swap = braid` under
`Symmetric`, not under bare `Braided`). Domain comments belong on the use site
or on a real composition — not on a synonym.

Duplicate definitions of the same unitor in a second module
(`TensorNetwork.Categorical` vs `Categorical.Linear`) are the same smell:
re-export or import; do not restate.

### Stay on the typed value; don’t unpack buffers

Avoid `toArray` / `unsafeFromArray` / `VS.!` loops to define core maps or
read scalars when a morphism, inner product, or `Sized`/`Tensor` constructor
suffices. Buffer unpacking is for IO, probes, and `*.Reference` — not for
naming the meaning of a linear map or a unit-object amplitude.

## Type classes (use sparingly)

Type classes are a **code stain** unless they earn their keep. Reach for them
only when necessary, for example:

- **Type-level recursion** on spines / HLists / type families (one scheme class;
  pass rank-2 algebras as ordinary functions — do not invent a class per walk)
- **Genuine overload** that cannot be a plain function or a shared composition
  (e.g. group-indexed `fuse` when U(1) and SU(2) share a caller API)

Do **not** add a class because OO / traits / interfaces would, or to name an
operation that has a single implementation. Prefer plain functions, rank-2
arguments, existing schemes (`HMap` / `MapSU2Mult1`-style walkers), and
`newtype` wrappers. Prefer **reusing** an existing class over inventing a new
one.

**OK:** empty-method classes that **bundle constraints** and carry **inductive
instances** over a type-level tree (`KnownToVObj` over `Obj`: Irrep / `:⊗:` /
`:⊕:`). That is not OO — a `ConstraintKinds` synonym cannot hold those
instances. Same idea as marker classes that add a law (`Symmetric`).

**OK:** forward `ConstraintKinds` synonyms that bundle known parts
(`KnownHomTrees a b` = `KnownFTrees a/b/(FuseFTrees a b)` + `FuseFTreesTermC`).
Prefer that over repeating the same four lines; do **not** invent a method
class (`CanFmove*`, `FilterTrivialC`) when a plain function plus `KnownFTrees`
(and `cmpNat` / singletons for spine case-splits) already states the obligation.

```haskell
-- BAD: new class for one algebra on an HList walk
class RPhase j1 j2 x where applyRPhase :: x -> x

-- GOOD: one spine walker + a plain function
mapSU2Mult1 (rPhaseOn @j1 @j2) hs
```

## Type-driven process ([Haskell Guide](https://haskell-docs.netlify.app/))

- Grow features top-down with honest `undefined` stubs; let the compiler infer
  stub types before filling them in.
- Debug type errors by substituting `undefined` to localize the hole; then ask
  for its type (`cabal repl` / `:t` / HLS).
- Prefer folds / named schemes over hand-rolled recursion and imperative
  `State` loops when the problem is a fold.
- Prefer existing typeclasses + `newtype` wrappers over inventing new classes
  (see **Type classes** above).
- Custom types enforce conceptual distinctions (`ChessSquare` ≠ `(Int,Int)`);
  keep that discipline for bonds / sites / morphisms.

## Incremental edits

- Prefer the smallest typed milestone that typechecks.
- One conceptual change per edit cycle when possible; verify with
  `cabal build quantum:lib:quantum` before stacking the next.
